const e="naadam-mongolia-three-manly-games",h=2025,a="There is no organiser rulebook, entry circular, fee list or registration procedure for the National Naadam in the sources that could be obtained, so this is the 'source with no rulebook' case: each sentence names its source and year, and previous-edition (2025) dates, venue and awards are Variables only. Read in full: the Montsame 2025 report, the ILO 2006 article, the Wikipedia article and the 2003 Law on the National Grand Naadam (Mongolian, translated). The 2003 law was replaced by a revised law adopted on 28 June 2022 and its current text could not be retrieved, so the lines marked '2003 law' must be checked against the law in force. A draft of the revised law reproduced in the Mongolian press (2022) was also seen; it was not copied because it is a draft. Not printed in any source: entry fees, registration steps and deadlines, a contact person or phone, the competition address beyond the stadium named in 2025, prize amounts, and age limits for wrestlers and archers. The template's evidence says horses go through formal pre-race registration and age verification and that riders must meet statutory minimum-age, registration, insurance and safety rules; the ILO article read here prints the minimum age of 7, the insurance rule (2003 law) and the protective gear decree, but no pre-race registration step. Winner names and donors are not copied. Template difference: the template has one group 'Open (Adult)' for ages 16 and over; in horse racing the riders are children, and wrestling is for men only. Knucklebone shooting (shagai), named in the 2025 report, is not in the template.",t={Title:"Rules & Criteria",Subtitle:"National Naadam of Mongolia, the Three Manly Games (Eriin Gurvan Naadam): wrestling, archery and horse racing.",NoticeTitle:"Please Read Before Registering",Notice:"No organiser circular, entry form or fee list for the National Naadam is published in the sources used here. The dates, venue and awards below are from the previous edition ({{PastEditionStart}} to {{PastEditionEnd}}, 2025) and are only a starting point. Check the dates, entry rules and registration steps with the organising committee.",Notes:["The three games are Mongolian wrestling (Bökh), archery and horse racing. Naadam is held in midsummer and was inscribed on the UNESCO Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2010 (Wikipedia).","Wrestling is for men only. Archery is open to men and women. In horse racing the riders are children.","Every statement on this page names the source and its year. The Mongolian law that governs Naadam was replaced by a revised law in 2022, so check the law in force."],Sections:[{Key:"general",Title:"About the Festival",Items:["Naadam ('games') is a traditional festival of Mongolia, held in midsummer. It is also called the 'eriin gurvan naadam', the three games of men: wrestling, horse racing and archery (Wikipedia).","The National Naadam is held every year in July (ILO 2006). The 2025 festival was held on {{PastEditionStart}} and {{PastEditionEnd}} and marked these anniversaries: {{PastEditionAnniversaries}} (Montsame 2025).","A national festival is made up of festivals at national, aimag (province), soum (district) and capital city levels (2003 law).","The festival is opened and closed by the President of Mongolia. In 2025 the festival closed with a closing announcement by the President just after the 9th round of wrestling (Montsame 2025; 2003 law).","In 2025 the National Naadam had wrestling, archery, knucklebone shooting (shagai) and horse racing games (Montsame 2025).","The festival is organised by the National Naadam organising committee. Under the 2003 law its chair is a member of the Government and its deputy chair is the Governor of the capital city, and it has sub-committees (2003 law).","A competitor must respect the audience and the festival, and keep the state ceremonies and traditions (2003 law)."]},{Key:"eligibility",Title:"Who Can Take Part",Items:["Wrestling: only men take part (Wikipedia).","Archery: both men and women take part (Wikipedia). In 2025 the national archery competition had a men's and a women's title (Montsame 2025).","Horse racing: the riders are children. A child must be 7 years or older to ride in a Naadam race (ILO 2006, citing the Law on the National Grand Naadam; 2003 law, 8.2).","A child rider must be insured against accidents (2003 law, 8.2 and 10.6).","A 2005 decree of the Deputy Prime Minister requires child jockeys to wear protective helmets, knee pads and elbow pads (ILO 2006).","Wrestling at the 2025 National Naadam had 512 wrestlers, holding ranks from soum to national titles (Montsame 2025).","Up to 1,000 horses from any part of Mongolia can be chosen to take part in the horse races (Wikipedia).","Previous edition (2025): participants came from across the country. The sources print no age limit for wrestlers and archers, no entry fee and no sign-up procedure."]},{Key:"registration",Title:"How to Register",Items:["The sources print no registration steps, entry form, deadline or fee for wrestlers, archers or horse owners. Ask the organising committee.","For horse racing, the owner (uyach) must have the child rider insured, and a horse must run only in its own age class (2003 law, 10.6).","A horse trainer's results count only for the person who was registered as the trainer with the horse racing sub-committee (2003 law, 8.8)."]},{Key:"levels",Title:"Rounds and Levels",Items:["Wrestling is a single-elimination tournament of 512 or 1,024 wrestlers, with nine or ten rounds (Wikipedia). 512 wrestlers wrestled in nine rounds in 2025 (Montsame 2025).","A 1,024-wrestler tournament is held in years when a big anniversary of the Great Mongol State and of the People's Revolution fall together; other years have 512 wrestlers (summary of the 2022 revised law as reported by Mongolian news; check the law).","Under the 2003 law an aimag festival had 128 wrestlers (256 in some years) and a soum festival had 64 wrestlers (128 in some years).","Horse racing is cross-country, with races of 15 to 30 km by age class (Wikipedia). The ILO (2006) printed 12 to 28 km depending on the horse's age. In 2025 there were five horse-age races (Montsame 2025).","The 2003 law names six horse age classes: azarga (stallion), ikh nas (mature horse), soyolon, khyazaalan, shudlen and daaga.","Archery is shot in a series of rounds (2003 law, 9.1)."]},{Key:"points",Title:"Titles, Ranks and Scoring",Items:["Wrestling has no score. A wrestler wins a round or is out (single elimination).","Wrestling state titles are given by the number of rounds won: Nachin (falcon) after five, Khartsaga (hawk) after six, Zaan (elephant) after seven, Garuda after eight, Arslan (lion) after nine. A winner of the tenth stage, or a two-time Arslan, is called Avarga (champion or giant) (Wikipedia; 2003 law 7.1).","In 2025 the wrestlers who won five rounds received the State Falcon (Ulsyn Nachin) title, and those who won six rounds the State Hawk (Ulsyn Khartsaga) title. The winner of the nine rounds received the State Lion (Ulsyn Arslan) title and the runner-up the State Garuda (Ulsyn Garid) title (Montsame 2025).","Archery: in 2025 the winners of the men's and women's competition each received the State Master (Ulsyn Mergen) title (Montsame 2025).","Under the 2003 law, at the National Naadam the archer in 3rd place receives the title State Gots archer (gots harvaach), 2nd place State Garamgai archer, and the winner State Mergen. A State Mergen who wins again receives State Dayan Mergen.","Horse racing: in 2025 child jockeys whose horses finished in the top five of each of the five horse-age races received gifts from the President (Montsame 2025). Under the 2003 law the horses placed 6th to 10th in all age classes are published to the public.","Horse owners earn titles from results: a national Honoured Owner (Aldart uyach) when a horse wins 4 times or is placed 8 times, a Senior Owner (Manlai uyach) at 6 wins or 12 places, and a Distinguished Senior Owner (Tod manlai uyach) at 10 wins or 20 places. At least 50 percent of the owner's horses must have won or placed in the stallion and mature classes. Owner titles are given at the Lunar New Year (Tsagaan Sar) (2003 law, 8.3 to 8.7)."]},{Key:"prizes",Title:"Awards",Items:["State titles are given by the President of Mongolia on the proposal of the organising committee. Aimag and soum titles are given by the aimag or soum Governor. The organising committee gives the badge of the title (2003 law, 6.1 and 6.3).","Previous edition (2025): {{PastEditionPrizes}}","The Government sets the amount of the badge and the prize money for wrestlers, horse owners, archers and child riders (2003 law, 11.1.5). The sources print no amounts.","Titles in the national sports are given only at the Naadam festivals (national, aimag and soum), not at other holidays, unless the law says otherwise (2003 law, 6.2)."]},{Key:"dates",Title:"Dates",Items:["The National Naadam is in July (ILO 2006). Previous edition: {{PastEditionStart}} to {{PastEditionEnd}} (Montsame 2025).","A Naadam for horse trainers was held on 13 July 2025 at {{HorseTrainersNaadamPlace}} (Montsame 2025)."]},{Key:"venue",Title:"Venue",Items:["Previous edition (2025): {{PastEditionVenue}}."]},{Key:"conduct",Title:"Conduct and Penalties",Items:["A wrestler, horse owner or archer who breaks the duties in the law can be: not allowed to take part or removed from that festival's games; refused the badge and prize of that festival; or barred from the festival for up to 2 years (2003 law, 14.1).","A wrestler in the last 16 and the top three archers must take a doping (stimulant) test. Refusing the test counts as using a stimulant. If the test shows it, the title and place won at that festival are taken away (2003 law, 10.2, 10.9 and 14.3).","A horse owner must not race a horse in a different age class and must not follow the horse or chase it on purpose (2003 law, 10.6).","An archer must follow the instructions of the judge (2003 law, 10.8).","Wrestling is untimed. A wrestler loses if any part of the body other than the feet or hands touches the ground (Wikipedia)."]},{Key:"complaints",Title:"Complaints",Items:["A wrestler, horse owner or archer can complain about a decision made during the festival (2003 law, 10.3, 10.5 and 10.7).","A complaint against a soum committee goes to the aimag committee. A complaint against an aimag or sub-committee decision goes to the organising committee. A decision of the organising committee can be taken to court (2003 law, 15)."]},{Key:"other",Title:"Other Games Named in the Sources",Items:["Knucklebone shooting (shagai harvaa) is a fourth game at the National Naadam. It is not part of the template (Montsame 2025).","The Naadam for horse trainers is held after the festival (Montsame 2025).","Youth wrestling and youth archery for children can be held at the National Naadam, and the organising committee gives titles to children who take part in them, on the proposal of the national sports federation (2003 law, 12.4.2)."]}],Documents:[{Title:"Montsame: National Naadam 2025 report",Url:"https://montsame.mn/en/read/374138"},{Title:"ILO: Mongolian child jockeys",Url:"https://www.ilo.org/resource/article/mongolian-child-jockeys-balancing-cultural-heritage-safety"}]},n=[],r=[],o=[{Title:"Wrestling (Bökh)",Source:"Wikipedia article Naadam; Montsame, National Naadam 2025 report (2025-07-13); Law on the National Grand Naadam, 2003 text (replaced by a revised law in 2022), article 7",Sections:{About:["Mongolian wrestling (Bökh). Only men take part.","Wrestlers meet in a single-elimination tournament of 512 or 1,024 wrestlers over nine or ten rounds."],TimeAndFormat:["Wrestling is not timed (Wikipedia)."],Rules:["A wrestler loses if any part of the body other than the feet or hands touches the ground.","When pairs are chosen, the wrestler with the greatest fame has the right to choose his opponent.","Wrestlers wear a two-piece costume: a tight shoulder vest (zodog) and shorts (shuudag).","Each wrestler has an 'encourager' (zasuul) who sings a song of praise after rounds three, five and seven.","Under the 2003 law, in rounds one, two and four wrestlers are matched by title and past record, and in the other rounds they choose their opponent in the same order. The 'choose by record' rule does not apply to a Darkhan Avarga."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:["State titles by rounds won: Nachin (falcon) after five, Khartsaga (hawk) after six, Zaan (elephant) after seven, Garuda after eight, Arslan (lion) after nine. The winner of the tenth stage, or a two-time Arslan, is Avarga.","Ranks used in this template: Nachin (Falcon), Zaan (Elephant), Arslan (Lion) and Avarga (Titan)."],Notes:["In 2025: 512 wrestlers, nine rounds ({{PastEditionWrestlers}})."]},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Mongolian wrestling (Bökh). Only men take part.
- Wrestlers meet in a single-elimination tournament of 512 or 1,024 wrestlers over nine or ten rounds.

## Time and format
- Wrestling is not timed (Wikipedia).

## Rules
- A wrestler loses if any part of the body other than the feet or hands touches the ground.
- When pairs are chosen, the wrestler with the greatest fame has the right to choose his opponent.
- Wrestlers wear a two-piece costume: a tight shoulder vest (zodog) and shorts (shuudag).
- Each wrestler has an 'encourager' (zasuul) who sings a song of praise after rounds three, five and seven.
- Under the 2003 law, in rounds one, two and four wrestlers are matched by title and past record, and in the other rounds they choose their opponent in the same order. The 'choose by record' rule does not apply to a Darkhan Avarga.

## Points
- State titles by rounds won: Nachin (falcon) after five, Khartsaga (hawk) after six, Zaan (elephant) after seven, Garuda after eight, Arslan (lion) after nine. The winner of the tenth stage, or a two-time Arslan, is Avarga.
- Ranks used in this template: Nachin (Falcon), Zaan (Elephant), Arslan (Lion) and Avarga (Titan).

## Notes for participants
- In 2025: 512 wrestlers, nine rounds ({{PastEditionWrestlers}}).`},{Title:"Archery",Source:"Wikipedia article Naadam; Montsame, National Naadam 2025 report (2025-07-13); Law on the National Grand Naadam, 2003 text (replaced by a revised law in 2022), article 9",Sections:{About:["Mongolian archery. Both men and women take part."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["The archers shoot in teams of ten. Each archer has four arrows and the team must hit 33 targets (surs).","Men shoot from 75 metres and women from 65 metres.","The targets (surs) are small woven or wooden cylinders placed on each other to form a wall three high, about eight inches high and five feet wide.","Knocking a sur out of the wall with an arrow counts as a hit. Knocking one out of the centre earns more points.","After each hit an official repairs the wall for the next attempt.","Archers traditionally wear national clothing (deel) and leather bracers up to the elbow on the outstretched arm."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:["The winners receive the titles of 'national marksman' and 'national markswoman' (State Master, Ulsyn Mergen)."],Notes:["The archery details come from Wikipedia and are not an organiser rule."]},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Mongolian archery. Both men and women take part.

## Rules
- The archers shoot in teams of ten. Each archer has four arrows and the team must hit 33 targets (surs).
- Men shoot from 75 metres and women from 65 metres.
- The targets (surs) are small woven or wooden cylinders placed on each other to form a wall three high, about eight inches high and five feet wide.
- Knocking a sur out of the wall with an arrow counts as a hit. Knocking one out of the centre earns more points.
- After each hit an official repairs the wall for the next attempt.
- Archers traditionally wear national clothing (deel) and leather bracers up to the elbow on the outstretched arm.

## Points
- The winners receive the titles of 'national marksman' and 'national markswoman' (State Master, Ulsyn Mergen).

## Notes for participants
- The archery details come from Wikipedia and are not an organiser rule.`},{Title:"Horse Racing",Source:"Wikipedia article Naadam; ILO article on Mongolian child jockeys (2006-09-22); Montsame, National Naadam 2025 report (2025-07-13); Law on the National Grand Naadam, 2003 text (replaced by a revised law in 2022), article 8",Sections:{About:["Mongolian horse racing is a cross-country race, not a short sprint. The riders are children."],TimeAndFormat:["Races are 15 to 30 km long by age class: for example two-year-old horses race 16 km and seven-year-olds 27 km (Wikipedia). The ILO (2006) printed 12 to 28 km."],Rules:["The race is run by horse age class. The 2003 law names six: azarga (stallion), ikh nas (mature horse), soyolon, khyazaalan, shudlen and daaga. Five horse-age races were run in 2025.","Up to 1,000 horses from any part of Mongolia can be chosen to take part.","The rider must be 7 years or older and insured against accidents.","Child jockeys must wear protective helmets, knee pads and elbow pads (2005 decree).","The owner (uyach) must not race a horse in a different age class and must not follow the horse or chase it on purpose."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:["The President gives gifts to the child jockeys whose horses finish in the top five of each horse-age race (2025).","Owner titles are earned by wins and places over time (see the awards on the main page)."],Notes:["The ILO (2006) says that ILO-backed forum talks asked for pre-race medical checks, insurance and a higher age for commercial racing. These are suggestions, not rules.","Template difference: the template lists 'Open (Adult)' for ages 16 and over. The riders in horse racing are children."]},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Mongolian horse racing is a cross-country race, not a short sprint. The riders are children.

## Time and format
- Races are 15 to 30 km long by age class: for example two-year-old horses race 16 km and seven-year-olds 27 km (Wikipedia). The ILO (2006) printed 12 to 28 km.

## Rules
- The race is run by horse age class. The 2003 law names six: azarga (stallion), ikh nas (mature horse), soyolon, khyazaalan, shudlen and daaga. Five horse-age races were run in 2025.
- Up to 1,000 horses from any part of Mongolia can be chosen to take part.
- The rider must be 7 years or older and insured against accidents.
- Child jockeys must wear protective helmets, knee pads and elbow pads (2005 decree).
- The owner (uyach) must not race a horse in a different age class and must not follow the horse or chase it on purpose.

## Points
- The President gives gifts to the child jockeys whose horses finish in the top five of each horse-age race (2025).
- Owner titles are earned by wins and places over time (see the awards on the main page).

## Notes for participants
- The ILO (2006) says that ILO-backed forum talks asked for pre-race medical checks, insurance and a higher age for commercial racing. These are suggestions, not rules.
- Template difference: the template lists 'Open (Adult)' for ages 16 and over. The riders in horse racing are children.`}],s=[],i=[{Key:"PastEditionStart",Kind:"date",Label:"Previous edition (2025): first day of the National Naadam",Value:"2025-07-11",Source:"Montsame, National Naadam 2025 report (2025-07-13)"},{Key:"PastEditionEnd",Kind:"date",Label:"Previous edition (2025): last day of the National Naadam",Value:"2025-07-12",Source:"Montsame, National Naadam 2025 report (2025-07-13)"},{Key:"PastEditionAnniversaries",Kind:"text",Label:"Previous edition (2025): anniversaries the festival marked",Value:"The 2234th anniversary of the establishment of Mongolian Statehood, the 819th anniversary of the Great Mongol Empire, the 114th anniversary of the Restoration of National Freedom and Independence, the 104th anniversary of the People's Revolution and the 35th anniversary of the Democratic Revolution",Source:"Montsame, National Naadam 2025 report (2025-07-13)"},{Key:"PastEditionWrestlers",Kind:"text",Label:"Previous edition (2025): wrestlers and rounds",Value:"512 wrestlers, holding ranks from soum (district) to national titles, in a tournament of nine rounds",Source:"Montsame, National Naadam 2025 report (2025-07-13)"},{Key:"PastEditionVenue",Kind:"address",Label:"Previous edition (2025): venue named in the report",Value:{Name:"Central Stadium",Address:"The 9th round of the wrestling tournament and the closing announcement were at the Central Stadium",City:"Ulaanbaatar, Mongolia",MapUrl:"",Directions:""},Source:"Montsame, National Naadam 2025 report (2025-07-13)"},{Key:"HorseTrainersNaadamPlace",Kind:"text",Label:"Previous edition (2025): Naadam for horse trainers on 13 July",Value:"Khui Doloon Khudag",Source:"Montsame, National Naadam 2025 report (2025-07-13) (Naadam for Horse trainers is taking place at Khui Doloon Khudag)"},{Key:"PastEditionPrizes",Kind:"prize",Label:"Previous edition (2025): awards presented by the President",Value:"Certificates and medals to the winners of wrestling, archery, knucklebone shooting and horse racing. Gifts to the child jockeys whose horses finished in the top five of each of the five horse-age races. A racehorse to the youngest new State Falcon title holder (an annual tradition).",Source:"Montsame, National Naadam 2025 report (2025-07-13)"},{Key:"Naadam2025Report",Kind:"news",Label:"News report of the previous edition",Value:[{Date:"2025-07-13",Title:"New champions crowned as Mongolia celebrates National Naadam",Text:"The National Naadam Festival was held on 11-12 July 2025. 512 wrestlers took part in the traditional wrestling tournament, which ended after nine rounds with the State Lion (Ulsyn Arslan) title and the State Garuda title for the runner-up. In national archery, men's and women's winners each earned the State Master (Ulsyn Mergen) title. Wrestlers who won six rounds received the State Hawk (Ulsyn Khartsaga) title and wrestlers who won five rounds the State Falcon (Ulsyn Nachin) title. The festival closed with a closing announcement by the President just after the 9th round of wrestling.",Url:"https://montsame.mn/en/read/374138"}],Source:"Montsame, National Naadam 2025 report (2025-07-13)"}],l=[{Claim:"Edition facts of the 2025 National Naadam (dates, anniversaries, 512 wrestlers, nine rounds, titles, President's awards, closing).",SourceType:"Secondary - state news agency report",Source:"Montsame, 'New Champions Crowned as Mongolia Celebrates National Naadam with Pride', 2025-07-13",Url:"https://montsame.mn/en/read/374138"},{Claim:"Child jockey rules (age 7 or older under the Law on the National Grand Naadam, 2005 decree on protective gear, race distances).",SourceType:"Secondary - international organisation article",Source:"ILO, 'Mongolian child jockeys - balancing cultural heritage with safety', 2006-09-22",Url:"https://www.ilo.org/resource/article/mongolian-child-jockeys-balancing-cultural-heritage-safety"},{Claim:"Description of the three games: wrestling structure and titles, archery teams and targets, horse racing distances.",SourceType:"Secondary - encyclopedia",Source:"Wikipedia, Naadam",Url:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naadam"},{Claim:"Legal frame of the National Naadam: festival levels, titles, horse age classes, child riders, duties, penalties, complaints (2003 text, replaced by a revised law in 2022).",SourceType:"Primary - Law of Mongolia (superseded text)",Source:"Law of Mongolia on the National Grand Naadam, 2003 text",Url:"https://legalinfo.mn/mn/detail/17"}],d={TemplateKey:e,Year:2025,Note:a,Page:t,ParticipantTypes:n,AgeGroups:r,Competitions:o,CompetitionAgeGroups:s,Variables:i,Evidence:l};export{r as AgeGroups,s as CompetitionAgeGroups,o as Competitions,l as Evidence,a as Note,t as Page,n as ParticipantTypes,e as TemplateKey,i as Variables,h as Year,d as default};
